FULL={
2:[['**A neural network makes one static decision, but real cognition flows in time.** A network is given an input, such as the pixels of a face or a spoken vowel, and it makes one decision about it. Language and speech keep changing from moment to moment, so one static decision cannot capture them.',
    'Take the sentence “The wolves that run around Catalina Island ___.” A speaker of English fills the gap with “are,” because the verb agrees with “wolves” however many words come between them. Real language skill depends on these long-distance relationships.',
    'Speech has them too. A listener can tell that a sentence is about to end because the speaker slows down, so the timing far back in the sentence is related to how it ends.',
    'The **sigmoid** carries two ideas. A neuron is either on or off, and people sort things into categories, treating two physically different things as the same and two very similar things as different.'],
   ['**An agentic system gives each LLM a different job, and the LLMs check each other.** The first LLM is told not to answer the question and to write an outline. The second writes a first draft from the outline. The third criticizes the draft, looking for spelling errors, grammar errors and flaws, and it can search the web to check that a reference exists.',
    'The draft goes back and forth between the critic and the writer, usually up to a limit of 3 or 4 rounds. A final LLM then writes the answer the user sees, which is why an answer can take a minute or two.',
    'A **hallucination** is an answer that sounds right but is made up, such as a legal reference that does not exist. Hallucination dropped enormously once LLMs were set up to check each other, although it has not disappeared.',
    'The systems have also grown. The neural networks studied so far have thousands of W’s, the earliest LLMs had hundreds of millions, and the largest current systems are thought to have trillions.']],
3:[['**Each head is its own neural network that makes a decision, and each layer has its own set of heads.** Imagine wanting to improve yourself and visiting five classes. In the first class, each student gives advice, and each student is a head.',
    'The advice from all the students is combined, which gives a better representation of each vector. The second class is the next layer, with its own heads, and the same thing happens again.',
    'This continues until the representations of the vectors are good enough to predict the next word.']],
4:[['**An LLM is a dynamical system in the same sense as the oscillator.** In the oscillator, knowing x gives the next v, and knowing v gives the next x, so the two numbers keep recreating each other.',
    'An LLM does the same kind of thing with tens of thousands of numbers, many times over. Each layer takes the current vectors and produces new vectors.',
    'This is the idea that ties the physical systems and the AI systems of the course together.']],
5:[['**The first LLMs worked on text, and they learned by having their W’s changed after every wrong guess.** Take “The cat that the dog chased ran up the old tree and sat on the ___.” A speaker of English expects “branch” or “mat,” and never “may,” “in” or “the.”',
    'An LLM starts with random W’s, so at first it predicts impossible words. After each guess the W’s change so that the prediction becomes more like the actual next word. This is repeated millions and billions of times, until the model has the same grasp of English that a speaker has.'],
   ['**Within about two years, the same design was used for speech, images and proteins, only by changing what the vectors stand for.** For speech, each vector stands for a small piece of a spectrogram, and the model predicts the next piece. A spectrogram is a sequence of vectors in time, just as a sentence is.',
    'For an image, which does not vary in time, the image is cut into patch 1, patch 2, patch 3 and so on. Each patch is treated as if it were a word, and the model predicts patch 16.',
    'For a protein, the sequence is made of 20 kinds of amino acid. The sequence decides how the protein folds into its 3D shape, and the shape decides what the protein does in the body. Predicting an amino acid from the others is the same game, and this work received the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.',
    'The layers and heads stayed the same in every case. Speech synthesis from before LLMs could be understood but sounded unnatural, and the LLM systems sound natural.'],
   ['**The same technology solving all of these problems supports the horizontal view of cognition.** A **verticalist** holds that vision, hearing and language are separate skills, each with its own part of the brain. The **horizontal** view, proposed by the neural network researchers of the 1980s, holds that the same computations are used in every skill.',
    'The brain areas for hearing and vision are in different places, but their cells are organized in the same way and have the same feedback loops. One design of layers with heads then solved language, speech, vision and protein folding.']],
9:[['**Guessing the next word is called the GPT task, and it is how these systems learn.** After “The chef cracks the,” there is a big probability for “egg,” smaller ones for “nut” and “code,” and hardly any chance of “the.”',
    'The weights are nudged by **backpropagation**, so the guesses get better and better.',
    '**Multimodal systems**, which handle text, speech and images together, are about two to three years old. People are multimodal too. A listener hears the speech, follows the words and watches the gestures, and combines all of them into one meaning.']],
10:[['**The three forms can be three versions of one message.** There are the words, there is the speech signal of someone saying “now crack the egg,” which is the pressure variation in front of the mouth, and there is an image of an egg that has been cracked. A multimodal agent understands how the sound, the words and the image relate.',
    'Before multimodal systems, each kind of model was trained by itself. A text model predicted “egg” from “now crack the.” A speech model had a small piece of the signal taken out and predicted the next piece. A vision model had a patch taken out and predicted the missing patch.']],
13:[['**The game played inside one modality can be played across modalities, because the same technology is used for all three.** The patches of an image are followed, in the same sequence, by the frames of speech, and then by the text vectors. The model is asked to predict the next word from all of these vectors, not only from the words before it.',
    'A system trained this way has many more W’s. Its W’s are no longer only about text, only about speech or only about vision. They are about how the image patches of an egg relate to the speech vectors of someone talking about eggs, and how both relate to the words.',
    'After training over and over on a great deal of speech, images and words, the W’s become truly multimodal. The system then knows the relationship between spoken language, written language and what is seen.',
    'It is still built from layers with heads, and from W’s doing inner products and sigmoids.']],
14:[['**The example comes from ordinary social media data.** Someone posts an image of three cute puppies, writes “three cute puppies” under it, and adds a video in which they say the same words. The same event is now present in all three modalities, so a system has something to train on.',
    'The W’s of these systems come from data like this, which people produced. For that reason some lawmakers argue that people should be paid for the data the systems were trained on, and this is being argued in court.']],
15:[['**The same post can train the system in three separate ways, and the first way hides a word.** The system is given a few of the words, such as “three cute,” together with all of the speech frames and all of the image patches.',
    'It has to predict the next text word, and its W’s change so that it predicts that word better.']],
16:[['**The second way hides part of the speech.** The system is given the patches of the image, the words, and half of the speech.',
    'It has to predict the next bit of speech, and it uses the speech frames, the text words and the image patches to do it.']],
17:[['**The third way hides a patch, and the words and the speech are what make the right guess likely.** The system is given the words, all of the speech, and some of the patches. The next patch is the third dog.',
    'From two dogs in the image alone, the next patch could be a kennel, a person or the sun in the background. But the text contains “three” and “puppies,” and the speech says “three puppies.”',
    'So the probability of a person, a house or a kennel is very low, and the highest probability is that a third puppy is there. The other modalities spell out what is in the image.']],
18:[['**One situation can train the system in every direction.** The same post is used to go from speech and vision to text, from text and vision to speech, and through all the other possibilities.',
    'This is repeated over many different posts, such as three factories with people standing in front of them.',
    '**W’s are knowledge.** After this training, the knowledge in the W’s is about the bond between several skills, which are speaking, hearing and seeing.',
    'The idea behind each step is simple. Someone who understood the text system replaced the words with frames of speech, or with patches of an image.']],
19:[['**A modality is like a sense, and language counts as one too.** At first there were LLMs for language alone, then for speech alone, for vision alone and for biology alone. Over the last three or four years, systems have intertwined all of them, and there are now agentic multimodal systems whose LLMs specialize in different things and talk to each other.',
    '**Biomedical multimodal LLMs** combine medical images such as MRI and ultrasound, the doctor’s notes, lab results and DNA or protein sequences in one system. Radiology was one of the first fields to change, and these systems have found tumors that doctors missed. They also make mistakes, as doctors do.',
    'An ordinary chat system already works this way. Asked a question in Armenian, it answered in Armenian, because its W’s know about a hundred languages, and it also takes images and speech.',
    'These systems need so many W’s partly because their knowledge is far wider than one person’s, covering about a thousand topics and a hundred languages.']],
}
